    {
      "id": "r20261026-a02",
      "title": "The Problem of E-Waste",
      "level": "Menengah atas",
      "min": 4,
      "body": [
        "Every year, people throw away millions of old phones, laptops, and televisions. This electronic waste, or e-waste, is one of the fastest-growing types of rubbish in the world.",
        "E-waste is dangerous because electronics contain harmful materials like lead and mercury. When old devices end up in landfills, these materials can leak into the soil and water. In some countries, people burn e-waste to get the valuable metals inside, but the smoke is poisonous.",
        "The good news is that most of an old device can be recycled. Many cities now have special collection points for electronics. Some companies also give discounts when you return your old phone. Before you throw a device away, check if it can be repaired, donated, or recycled. Small choices like these can make a big difference."
      ],
      "gloss": {
        "waste": "limbah",
        "throw away": "membuang",
        "harmful": "berbahaya",
        "landfills": "tempat pembuangan",
        "leak": "merembes",
        "valuable": "berharga",
        "poisonous": "beracun",
        "donated": "disumbangkan"
      },
      "qs": [
